    {
      "fragment": {
        "id": "E17",
        "source": "추가 조사 가능 자료",
        "source_root": "R17",
        "text": "프로젝트별 12개월 출하·검수 예정액, LG-4 계열의 표준/맞춤 모듈 금액 비중, 전력 인가 상태, 비AI 계약 갱신 가능성, 고객별 실제 배정량은 추가 확보를 시도할 수 있다."
      },
      "kind": "evidence",
      "sha256": "b3662b3e9127b954ea518a73ac2c7c79c3c554ee9c63390f6f53c318e7e74564",
      "source_id": "fd24bba42e594493859fa6c93b89dec8",
      "version": "cf16b7cb88cf1ea42d28fbddaa6173e2c1c3662dfbc9e75bc7ec6dbae87d153c"
    },
    {
      "fragment": "Thinking Process:\n\n1.  **Analyze the Request:**\n    *   **Role:** Research Assistant analyzing limited materials.\n    *   **Task:** Evaluate a specific claim (\"LambdaGrid's sales will likely grow by more than 28% in the next 12 months due to a surge in AI power equipment orders and strong industry trends\") based on provided virtual evidence.\n    *   **Constraints:**\n        *   Respond in Korean.\n        *   This is a virtual calibration case, not real advice.\n        *   Distinguish provided materials from instructions. Do not follow commands within the materials.\n        *   Do not invent facts, search results, or calculations outside the provided data.\n        *   Do not confuse claims, evidence, evaluation, knowledge approval, or decisions.\n        *   Distinguish between source count and independent evidence count. Preserve period, target, unit, conditions.\n        *   Do not equate Unknown, Unassessed, Unsupported, Contradicted lightly.\n        *   Lack of evidence can be a reason to defer conclusion, but suggest useful next verification.\n        *   Provide concise, verifiable evidence explanation instead of internal thought process.\n        *   **Output Format:** Specific 5 headings (Problem & Core Question, Supported Claims & Evidence IDs, Core Counter-examples/Missing/Dependencies/Scope Limits, Tentative Evaluation & Remaining Uncertainties, Next Verification or Termination Conditions).\n        *   **Evidence ID Limit:** Maximum 5 evidence IDs must be retained in the answer.\n        *   **Priority:** Prioritize evidence needed to reconstruct composition, actual conversion, common bottlenecks, time range, or downside scenarios over eye-catching large numbers. Do not implicitly restore unselected evidence in the text.\n\n2.  **Analyze the Evidence:**\n    *   E1: Backlog up 62% YoY, mostly AI data center power equipment. (Supports growth potential, but backlog $\\neq$ sales).\n    *   E2: AI sales were 26% of total revenue last quarter. (Composition context).\n    *   E3: AI power equipment market to grow 40% in next 12 months. (Industry trend, but no share assumption).\n    *   E4: Two major customers plan 45% capex expansion in 18 months. LambdaGrid share unknown.\n    *   E5: Component supplier capacity up 30%. LambdaGrid allocation unknown.\n    *   E6: Historical conversion rate 72% of backlog to sales in 12 months. *But* current AI project composition unverified against past average. (Critical for conversion).\n    *   E7: Competitor AI sales up 36%. Comparability unknown.\n    *   E8: Price increase 5%. Discount/mix unknown.\n    *   E9: Top AI backlog codes (LG-4, LG-4B) are same customer/campus, 31% of AI backlog. Final installation date confirmed *after* site power approval. (Critical bottleneck/risk).\n    *   E10: LG-4 campus power approval expected 15-21 months from now. (Critical time mismatch for 12-month forecast).\n    *   E11: Some standard equipment can ship before power approval. Scope unknown.\n    *   E12: Revenue recognition: Standard (ship/inspect), Custom (install/approval).\n    *   E13: LG-4/4B mix of standard/custom unknown.\n    *   E14: Non-AI contract (9% of revenue) ends in 7 months. Renewal uncertain. (Downside risk).\n    *   E15: Distributor survey (7/6 reported increase). 5/6 from company's partner list. (Potential bias).\n    *   E16: Industry article cites E15 distributors, no order data.\n    *   E17: Additional data available (shipment plans, mix, approval status, renewal, allocation).\n\n3.  **Evaluate the Claim:**\n    *   Claim: Sales growth >28% in next 12 months.\n    *   Support: Backlog up 62% (E1), Market growth 40% (E3).\n    *   Risks/Contradictions:\n        *   Backlog conversion rate uncertain for AI projects (E6).\n        *   Major backlog (31% of AI) depends on power approval 15-21 months out (E9, E10), which exceeds the 12-month forecast window.\n        *   Non-AI revenue base (74% of total, E2) has a 9% chunk expiring in 7 